---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Backfield"
  description="Backfield turns journalism into data you can build with."
>
  <article class="backfield">
    <section
      class="product-carousel"
      data-carousel
      aria-label="Backfield product screenshots"
      aria-roledescription="carousel"
    >
      <div class="carousel-stage">
        <figure class="carousel-slide is-active" data-carousel-slide aria-hidden="false">
          <img
            src="/images/backfield/carousel-1.png"
            alt="Agate flow builder connecting article input to entity extraction, geocoding, metadata and JSON output"
            width="1800"
            height="895"
          />
          <figcaption>Build custom extraction and enrichment workflows in Agate.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="carousel-slide" data-carousel-slide aria-hidden="true" hidden>
          <img
            src="/images/backfield/carousel-2.png"
            alt="Agate places review interface showing highlighted source evidence beside a map and geocoded locations"
            width="1800"
            height="903"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Review and geocode extracted places.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="carousel-slide" data-carousel-slide aria-hidden="true" hidden>
          <img
            src="/images/backfield/carousel-3.png"
            alt="Agate people review interface showing article evidence and extracted people with roles and affiliations"
            width="1800"
            height="891"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Inspect people, their roles, quotes and supporting evidence.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="carousel-slide" data-carousel-slide aria-hidden="true" hidden>
          <img
            src="/images/backfield/carousel-4.png"
            alt="Stylebook geography interface showing a maintained neighborhood boundary on a map of Chicago"
            width="1800"
            height="904"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Maintain canonical entities, geography and connections in Stylebook.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="carousel-slide" data-carousel-slide aria-hidden="true" hidden>
          <img
            src="/images/backfield/carousel-5.png"
            alt="API Playground showing geographic search of locations with a bounding box on a map"
            width="1800"
            height="872"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Explore and query structured coverage data through the Backfield API.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <header class="hero">
      <h1>Backfield turns journalism into data you can build with.</h1>
      <nav class="hero-actions" aria-label="Backfield links">
        <a class="button" href="https://try.backfield.news" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Try a demo</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/localangle/backfield" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get the code <span aria-hidden="true">›</span></a>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://docs.backfield.news" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the docs <span aria-hidden="true">›</span></a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <section class="introduction">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <p class="lede">
        Beyond the headline and the lede, every news story is full of quotes, anecdotes,
        people, places, facts and connections that are valuable beyond the story itself.
        Backfield is an open platform that captures and enriches that information — turning
        the journalism you produce every day into data you can build with.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="capabilities">
      <h2>From articles<br />to structured knowledge</h2>
      <p class="capabilities-intro">
        We tend to think about the article as the atomic unit of journalism. Backfield goes
        deeper, structuring entities and concepts within each story so they can be connected
        across your coverage and put to work in new ways.
      </p>
      <div class="capability-grid">
        <article>
          <p class="capability-number">01</p>
          <h3>Extract</h3>
          <p>
            Identify people, places, organizations, quotes and events — or define
            custom extractions for things like recipes, obituaries and other story forms.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <p class="capability-number">02</p>
          <h3>Enrich</h3>
          <p>
            Geocode places, resolve entities to canonical records, attach geographic
            boundaries and add newsroom-specific context.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <p class="capability-number">03</p>
          <h3>Describe</h3>
          <p>
            Classify stories based on subject, user needs, story forms and other metadata,
            then create embeddings to enable semantic search and similarity.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <p class="capability-number">04</p>
          <h3>Build</h3>
          <p>
            Query your coverage by person, place, topic or meaning to power search,
            feeds, newsletters, analytics and other products.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="use-cases">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it’s used</p>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2>Create more value from the journalism you already produce.</h2>
          <p class="use-cases-intro">
            We’ve developed many of these ideas through experiments with working news
            organizations. Consider them just a few examples of what's possible when the
            information within your journalism is available as structured data.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="use-case-grid">
        <article>
          <h3>Build local products</h3>
          <p>
            Create feeds for neighborhoods, suburbs and towns; build local newsletters
            and other experiences based on the places mentioned in your reporting.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Understand your coverage</h3>
          <p>
            Map your reporting, audit the sources you quote, and see how your report
            might appear in the eyes of the communities you cover.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Turn narratives into interactives</h3>
          <p>
            Extract custom information from articles, such as ingredients from recipes, or
            restaurants from restaurant roundups, and use that data to build new story forms.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Give new life to your archives</h3>
          <p>
            Enable geographic, semantic and entity-based search across your archive; create
            a community knowledge graph that can inform your reporting.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Enrich your analytics</h3>
          <p>
            Add custom metadata like subjects, user needs and story forms to your articles,
            then use those to ask more nuanced questions about how your coverage performs.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Support new revenue</h3>
          <p>
            Tell your story to advertisers and funders, showing how your coverage intersects
            with their needs and interests.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="use-cases-more">
        See more experiments and examples in the
        <a href="https://github.com/localangle/backfield-cookbook">Backfield Cookbook</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="how-it-works">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
        <h2>One platform,<br />three applications</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="product-list">
        <article>
          <h3>Agate</h3>
          <p>
            Build workflows that turn stories into structured data, with a suite of editorial
            review tools to keep humans in the loop.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Stylebook</h3>
          <p>
            Create, maintain, connect and enrich canonical records for the people, places
            and organizations featured in your coverage.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Backfield API</h3>
          <p>
            Query the resulting data to power newsroom tools, audience-facing products
            and other applications.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p>
        <h2>Questions about Backfield</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>Who is Backfield for?</summary>
          <p>
            Backfield is for any news organization that wants to explore how structured
            journalism can benefit their business or their mission.
          </p>
          <p>
            The platform is free to use under an Apache 2.0 license. If you don’t have
            engineering capacity or technical expertise, Local Angle offers several hosting 
            options and is happy to work with you to build products based on the data you create.
          </p>
          <p>
            For large organizations, Backfield is engineered to work at scale – capable of
            processing thousands of articles on dedicated and horizontally scalable
            infrastructure. You’re welcome to host it yourself, or Local Angle can do it
            for you.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How do I get started using Backfield?</summary>
          <p>
            Backfield is an open source platform, and you are welcome to run it
            locally or deploy it yourself by following the directions <a href="https://github.com/localangle/backfield">here</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Local Angle also offers hosting, onboarding and consulting services if
            you would like to run the platform without technical overhead. If you
            are interested in managed hosting, sign up for <a href="https://forms.gle/4TsNHXGZqxmJN4Zw9">the waitlist</a>.
          </p>
          <p>  
            Contact <strong>backfield@localangle.co</strong> if you have any questions.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which models does it support?</summary>
          <p>
            Backfield is model-agnostic. Out of the box, it works with the broad range
            of hosted and open models supported by the
            <a href="https://models.litellm.ai/">LiteLLM ecosystem</a>, including models
            from OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, xAI and popular open-weights alternatives.
            It can also be configured to use self-hosted or proprietary models.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What about privacy? Who gets access to our data?</summary>
          <p>
            Backfield gives you control over where your data goes. When a workflow uses
            an external language model, geocoder or search service, the information
            needed for that step is sent to that provider. What happens next is
            governed by that provider’s privacy and data-retention policies.
          </p>
          <p>
            Many commercial model APIs explicitly do not use customer API data to train 
            their models, but policies vary by provider and service. Backfield can
            also be configured to use self-hosted or proprietary models when tighter
            data controls are required.
          </p>
          <p>
            We can work with your organization to choose providers and deployment
            options that balance between accuracy, privacy and security.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can there be humans in the loop?</summary>
          <p>
            Absolutely. Like all automated systems, Backfield will make some mistakes 
            — whether that’s geocoding a place incorrectly or tagging an article with
            metadata you might not agree with.
          </p>
          <p>
            To what extent you need to correct those mistakes depends on your product
            needs. The system generally does a pretty good job, and if you can tolerate
            some errors around the edges, you may decide you don’t need human review at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            But Backfield data is at its best when it is at least occasionally reviewed by
            reporters and editors. There are a number of tools in the system designed to
            make that process quick and easy. And we can also do things like integrate
            with Slack to notify reporters and editors when there’s something they may
            want to check and correct.
          </p>
          <p>
            The system gets faster and more accurate if you are able to maintain a clean
            and reliable Stylebook, so any effort you put into cleaning your data is paid
            back as time goes on.
          </p>
          <p>
            At the end of the day, the level of human involvement you need is up to you.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much does it cost to operate?</summary>
            <p>
              Backfield itself is open source, so there is no software license fee to run
              it yourself. Your costs are whatever infrastructure, model APIs and external
              services your workflows require.
            </p>
            <p>
              For a mid-sized regional publisher processing 30–50 stories per day, costs for
              AI models, external geocoders and othe services might typically run a few hundred
              dollars per month, depending on the workflows and models you choose.
            </p>
            <p>
              Infrastructure costs vary widely depending on how you deploy Backfield. Lightweight
              deployments can be inexpensive; production systems requiring dedicated resources,
              redundancy, monitoring, backups and higher throughput will cost more.
            </p>
            <p>
              Local Angle also offers managed hosting for organizations that don't want to deploy
              and operate the system themselves. Managed pricing includes the infrastructure as well
              as deployment, maintenance, monitoring, upgrades and support. <a href="https://forms.gle/4TsNHXGZqxmJN4Zw9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sign up to the waitlist</a>
              if you are interested.
            </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How do you load stories into Backfield?</summary>
          <p>
            Backfield can ingest stories in several ways. A dedicated full-text RSS or
            JSON feed is usually the simplest, but it can also scrape articles directly
            from your website or connect to another source you provide.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does it work for things that aren’t journalism?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes, with some adaptation. Backfield is designed around the conventions and
            structure of news articles, so its default extractions, metadata and workflows
            are built with journalism in mind.
          </p>
          <p>
            But the underlying system is flexible. With different prompts, schemas and
            workflows, Backfield has also been used to process civic information and
            other kinds of documents. If you have a use case outside journalism,
            <a href="/work-with-us">we’d be interested in exploring it with you</a>.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hosted">
      <div class="section-copy">
        <h2>Want to try Backfield without hosting it yourself?</h2>
        <p>
          Local Angle operates a hosted version of Backfield for organizations
          that want to adopt the platform but not manage its infrastructure.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a class="button" href="https://forms.gle/4TsNHXGZqxmJN4Zw9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Join the hosted Backfield waitlist <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta">
      <h2>Put your coverage to work.</h2>
      <p>
        <a class="button" href="https://try.backfield.news" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Try a demo</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/localangle/backfield" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get the code</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://docs.backfield.news" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the docs</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="/work-with-us" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Talk with us <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-carousel]").forEach((carousel) => {
    const slides = Array.from(carousel.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-carousel-slide]"));
    const stage = carousel.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".carousel-stage");
    const rotationDelay = 4000;
    let active = 0;
    let rotationTimer: number | undefined;
    const reducedMotion = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;

    const showSlide = (index: number) => {
      active = (index + slides.length) % slides.length;

      slides.forEach((slide, slideIndex) => {
        const isActive = slideIndex === active;
        slide.classList.toggle("is-active", isActive);
        slide.setAttribute("aria-hidden", String(!isActive));
      });
    };

    const stopRotation = () => {
      window.clearTimeout(rotationTimer);
      rotationTimer = undefined;
    };

    const startRotation = () => {
      stopRotation();
      if (reducedMotion || document.hidden) return;
      rotationTimer = window.setTimeout(() => {
        showSlide(active + 1);
        startRotation();
      }, rotationDelay);
    };

    document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", startRotation);

    slides.forEach((slide) => { slide.hidden = false; });
    stage?.classList.add("is-ready");
    showSlide(0);
    startRotation();
  });
</script>

<style>
  .backfield {
    --backfield-wide: 54rem;
    margin-top: -2.5rem;
  }

  .hero {
    max-width: var(--backfield-wide);
    margin: 0 auto 6rem;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .hero h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 3.8vw, 3.3rem);
    font-weight: 420;
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .hero-actions {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 2rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .product-carousel {
    width: min(100%, var(--backfield-wide));
    margin: 0 auto 2.5rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .carousel-stage.is-ready {
    display: grid;
  }

  .carousel-slide {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .carousel-slide[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .carousel-stage.is-ready .carousel-slide {
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    transform: translateY(0.65rem) scale(0.992);
    transition:
      opacity 650ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
      transform 650ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
      visibility 0s linear 650ms;
    will-change: opacity, transform;
  }

  .carousel-stage.is-ready .carousel-slide.is-active {
    z-index: 1;
    opacity: 1;
    visibility: visible;
    transform: translateY(0) scale(1);
    transition-delay: 0s;
  }

  .carousel-slide img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 0.35rem;
  }

  .carousel-slide figcaption,
  .detail-image figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.66rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .carousel-stage.is-ready .carousel-slide {
      transform: none;
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  @media (max-height: 750px) and (min-width: 761px) {
    .product-carousel {
      width: min(100%, 42rem);
      padding-top: 0.5rem;
    }
  }

  .introduction,
  .capabilities,
  .how-it-works,
  .use-cases,
  .faq,
  .hosted,
  .cta {
    width: min(100%, var(--backfield-wide));
    margin-right: auto;
    margin-left: auto;
  }

  .introduction {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr;
    gap: 2rem;
    padding-bottom: 5rem;
  }

  .eyebrow,
  .capability-number {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .lede {
    max-width: 41rem;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.2vw, 1.8rem);
    line-height: 1.32;
    letter-spacing: -0.018em;
  }

  .capabilities,
  .how-it-works,
  .use-cases,
  .faq,
  .hosted {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: 4rem 0 5rem;
  }

  .hosted .section-copy {
    max-width: 36rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .hosted h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.5vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 420;
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  .hosted p {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    color: #4d4840;
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 1.6vw, 1.2rem);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .hosted p:last-child {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .capabilities > h2,
  .section-heading h2 {
    max-width: 34rem;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.5vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 420;
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  .capabilities-intro {
    max-width: 36rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    color: #4d4840;
    font-size: 1.06rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .capability-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    margin-top: 3rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .capability-grid article {
    padding: 1.4rem 1.4rem 0 0;
  }

  .capability-grid article + article {
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .capability-grid h3,
  .product-list h3,
  .use-case-grid h3 {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0.45rem;
    font-size: 1.18rem;
  }

  .capability-grid article > p:last-child,
  .product-list p,
  .use-case-grid p {
    margin: 0;
    color: #4d4840;
    font-size: 0.96rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .section-heading {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr;
    gap: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .section-copy {
    max-width: 34rem;
  }

  .section-copy h2 {
    max-width: none;
  }

  .use-cases-intro {
    max-width: 32rem;
    margin: 1.75rem 0 0;
    color: #4d4840;
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 1.6vw, 1.2rem);
    line-height: 1.55;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .use-cases-more {
    margin: 2rem 0 0 12rem;
    color: #4d4840;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
  }

  .product-list {
    margin-left: 12rem;
  }

  .product-list article {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    padding: 1.5rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .product-list article:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .detail-image {
    margin: 3rem 0 0 12rem;
  }

  .detail-image img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 0.35rem;
  }

  .product-list h3 {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .use-case-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    margin-left: 12rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .use-case-grid article {
    min-height: 11rem;
    padding: 1.5rem 1.75rem 1.75rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .use-case-grid article:nth-child(even) {
    padding-left: 1.75rem;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .use-case-grid h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .faq-list {
    margin-left: 12rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .faq-list details {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .faq-list summary {
    position: relative;
    padding: 1.2rem 2rem 1.2rem 0;
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    font-size: 1.06rem;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .faq-list summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  .faq-list summary::after {
    content: "+";
    position: absolute;
    right: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  .faq-list details[open] summary::after {
    content: "−";
  }

  .faq-list details p {
    max-width: 36rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0 2rem 1.4rem 0;
    color: #4d4840;
  }

  .cta {
    padding: 5rem 0 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .cta h2 {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    font-weight: 420;
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
  }

  .cta p {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

